Existing open-source software covers the combination of probit models and
preference heterogeneity only in part, as Table~\ref{tab:software} summarizes.
The \proglang{R} packages \pkg{mlogit} \citep{Croissant2020}, \pkg{gmnl}
\citep{Sarrias2017}, \pkg{logitr} \citep{Helveston2023}, and \pkg{apollo}
\citep{Hess2019}, as well as \pkg{Biogeme} \citep{Bierlaire2020, Bierlaire2025}
for \proglang{Python} \citep{VanRossum2009}, model heterogeneity in logit models
with a mixing distribution or a number of classes that the analyst fixes in
advance, by maximum (simulated) likelihood or, in the case of \pkg{apollo} and
\pkg{Biogeme}, also by Bayesian estimation. \pkg{bayesm} \citep{Rossi2025}
relaxes this restriction through finite and Dirichlet process mixtures of
normals, but only for the logit model. For the probit model, in contrast, the
multinomial models of \pkg{mlogit}, \pkg{bayesm}, and \pkg{MNP}
\citep{Imai2005b, Imai2026} have fixed coefficients, and \pkg{apollo},
\pkg{Biogeme}, and \pkg{Rchoice} \citep{Sarrias2016} offer random coefficients
for binary and ordered responses only. The frequentist counterpart of
\pkg{RprobitB} is \pkg{Rprobit} \citep{Bauer2024}. It estimates multinomial
probit models with normal mixing distributions by maximum likelihood or by the
maximum approximate composite marginal likelihood approach of \citet{Bhat2011},
with analytic approximations of the normal probabilities such as the Solow--Joe
approximation. To the best of our knowledge, \pkg{RprobitB} is the only package
that estimates multinomial probit models with random coefficients and correlated
errors in a Bayesian framework, and the only one that infers the number of
latent classes of such a model from the data. The package is available from the
Comprehensive \proglang{R} Archive Network (CRAN) at
\url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=RprobitB}, and its development version
is hosted at \url{https://github.com/loelschlaeger/RprobitB}.

The article proceeds as follows. Section~\ref{sec:model} defines the probit
model with random coefficients and latent classes. In
Section~\ref{sec:estimation}, we introduce the package's estimation routine
\fct{fit} and describe the prior distributions and the Gibbs sampler.
Section~\ref{sec:interpretation} interprets the posterior of a fitted model.
Section~\ref{sec:heterogeneity} models preference heterogeneity through random
coefficients and latent classes, whose number is fixed or inferred from the
data. Section~\ref{sec:ordered_ranked} treats ordered and ranked responses,
Sections~\ref{sec:prediction} and~\ref{sec:evaluation} cover prediction,
residuals, and model comparison, and Section~\ref{sec:summary} concludes the
paper.

\section{The probit model} \label{sec:model}

Assume that we observe the choices of $N$ deciders, where decider
$n = 1, \dots, N$ faces $T_n \geq 1$ choice occasions. At occasion
$t = 1, \dots, T_n$, the decider chooses among a finite set of $J \geq 2$
mutually exclusive and exhaustive alternatives \citep[Chapter~2]{Train2009}. The
case $T_n = 1$ for all $n$ corresponds to a cross section, and $T_n > 1$ to a
panel data set, which may be unbalanced. With $J = 2$, the model is the binary
probit model.

\subsection{Utilities and choices} \label{sec:model:utility}

Every alternative $j = 1, \dots, J$ provides decider $n$ at occasion $t$ with a
latent utility
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:utility}
  U_{ntj} = X_{ntj}^\top \theta_n + \varepsilon_{ntj},
\end{equation}
%
where $X_{ntj} \in \mathbb{R}^P$ collects the covariates and $\theta_n \in
\mathbb{R}^P$ the coefficients of decider $n$. The error vector
$\varepsilon_{nt} = (\varepsilon_{nt1}, \dots, \varepsilon_{ntJ})^\top \sim
\mathrm{N}_J(0, \Sigma)$ is multivariate normal with a $J \times J$ covariance
matrix $\Sigma$ and independent across deciders and occasions. The decider
chooses the available alternative with the largest utility,
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:link}
  y_{nt} = \operatorname*{argmax}_{j \in \mathcal{J}_{nt}} U_{ntj},
\end{equation}
%
where $y_{nt} = j$ denotes the event that decider $n$ chooses alternative $j$ at
occasion $t$, and $\mathcal{J}_{nt} \subseteq \{1, \dots, J\}$ is the set of
alternatives that are available at this occasion.

\subsection{Covariate types and the model formula} \label{sec:model:formula}

Covariates differ in whether their values and their coefficients vary across
alternatives. The price of a product is alternative-specific and usually has the
same coefficient in every utility. The income of a buyer is constant across
alternatives and can only enter through alternative-specific coefficients. Like
the price, the travel time varies across alternatives, but its coefficient may
differ between a car, which the traveler drives, and a train, in which the
traveler can read or work. To distinguish these three types, we write
(\ref{eq:utility}) as
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:utility_gen}
  U_{ntj} = \theta_{j}^{0} + A_{ntj}^\top \theta^{A} + B_{nt}^\top
  \theta_{j}^{B} + C_{ntj}^\top \theta_{j}^{C} + \varepsilon_{ntj},
\end{equation}
%
where the decider index of the coefficients is suppressed. The covariates
$A_{ntj}$ and $C_{ntj}$ depend on the alternative, while $B_{nt}$ depends on the
decider and the occasion only. The coefficient $\theta^{A}$ is the same for all
alternatives, whereas $\theta_{j}^{B}$ and $\theta_{j}^{C}$ are
alternative-specific. The intercepts $\theta_{j}^{0}$ are the
alternative-specific constants (ASCs). \pkg{RprobitB} specifies
(\ref{eq:utility_gen}) through a \class{formula} object of the form
\code{choice ~ A | B | C}, where \code{choice} is the response variable and
\code{A}, \code{B}, and \code{C} are the covariates of the three types. For
example, the formula \code{choice ~ price | income | time} gives the price one
coefficient for all alternatives, and the income and the travel time one
coefficient per alternative. The notation follows \pkg{mlogit} and builds on the
multi-part formulas of the \pkg{Formula} package \citep{Zeileis2010}. ASCs are
included by default and removed by \code{0} in the second part, as in
\code{choice ~ A | 0 | C}. We call every coefficient of
(\ref{eq:utility_gen}) an effect. The package labels every effect by its
covariate and, if the coefficient is alternative-specific, by the alternative.
For the formula above and the alternatives bus, car, and train, the labels
include \code{price}, \code{income_car}, \code{time_bus}, and \code{ASC_car}.

\subsection{Normalization} \label{sec:model:normalization}

Utilities are identified only up to level and scale. Adding the same constant to
all $U_{ntj}$ or multiplying all of them by a positive number leaves the choice
behavior implied by (\ref{eq:link}) unchanged
\citep[Chapter~2]{Train2009}. We normalize the level by taking differences with
respect to a base alternative $k$,
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:utility_diff}
  \tilde{U}_{ntj} = \tilde{X}_{ntj}^\top \theta_n + \tilde{\varepsilon}_{ntj},
  \qquad j \neq k,
\end{equation}
%
with $\tilde{U}_{ntj} = U_{ntj} - U_{ntk}$, $\tilde{X}_{ntj} = X_{ntj} -
X_{ntk}$, and $\tilde{\varepsilon}_{ntj} = \varepsilon_{ntj} -
\varepsilon_{ntk}$. The vector $\tilde{\varepsilon}_{nt}$ of the $J - 1$ error
differences is multivariate normal with mean zero and a $(J - 1) \times (J - 1)$
covariance matrix $\tilde{\Sigma}$. Differencing also shows that only the
differences of the ASCs and of the type \code{B} coefficients from those of the
base are identified. The base coefficients $\theta_{k}^{0}$ and $\theta_{k}^{B}$
are hence fixed at zero, and the remaining ones are interpreted relative to the
base, which is the first alternative in alphabetical order unless the argument
\code{base} of \fct{fit} names another one.

The scale is normalized by fixing one parameter, by default the error variance
of the first utility difference, $\tilde{\Sigma}_{11} = 1$. The argument
\code{scale} of \fct{fit} selects a different normalization, for example
\code{scale = c(price = -1)}, which fixes a coefficient instead of a variance
and measures every other coefficient in units of the fixed one. With a price
coefficient of $-1$, every other coefficient has a direct willingness-to-pay
interpretation, as in the electricity example of
Section~\ref{sec:random_coefficients}.

\subsection{Random coefficients} \label{sec:model:random}

Preferences can differ between deciders \citep{BenAkiva1993}, and the repeated
choices of a decider are informative about the coefficients of this decider
\citep{Allenby1998}. \pkg{RprobitB} therefore splits the coefficient vector into
$P_f$ fixed effects $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}^{P_f}$ that are common to all
deciders and $P_r$ random effects with a decider-specific value. The random
effects are functions of a latent vector $\beta_n \in \mathbb{R}^{P_r}$ that is
drawn independently for every decider from a multivariate normal distribution,
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:random}
  \beta_n \sim \mathrm{N}_{P_r}(b, \Omega), \qquad \theta_n = \big(\alpha^\top,
  g(\beta_n)^\top\big)^\top,
\end{equation}
%
independently of the covariates and the errors. The mean vector $b$ and the
covariance matrix $\Omega$ of this distribution are parameters of the model. The
map $g$ acts componentwise and determines the mixing distribution of each effect
$p = 1, \dots, P_r$,
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:mixing_map}
  g_p(\beta_{np}) =
  \begin{cases}
    \beta_{np} & \text{for a normal effect,} \\
    \exp(\beta_{np}) & \text{for a log-normal effect,} \\
    -\exp(\beta_{np}) & \text{for a negative log-normal effect.}
  \end{cases}
\end{equation}
%
A normal effect admits coefficients of either sign, distributed as
$\mathrm{N}(b_p, \Omega_{pp})$ over the population. A log-normal effect
restricts the coefficient to positive values, with median $\exp(b_p)$ and mean
$\exp(b_p + \Omega_{pp} / 2)$ over the population. A negative log-normal effect
is the negative of a log-normal variable and is appropriate when theory implies
a negative sign, for example of a price coefficient. A correlated effect has
free covariances in $\Omega$ with the other correlated effects, whereas an
uncorrelated effect has zeros in its row and column of $\Omega$ apart from the
variance on the diagonal. Table~\ref{tab:mixing} lists the six resulting mixing
distributions with the codes by which the argument \code{random_effects} of
\fct{fit} requests them, as in
\code{random_effects = c(price = "cln-", time = "cn")}. For log-normal effects,
$b$, $\Omega$, and the individual coefficients $\beta_n$ are reported on the
latent normal scale of (\ref{eq:random}).

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\hline
Code & Distribution & Correlated & Coefficient $g_p(\beta_{np})$ \\
\hline
\code{"cn"}   & normal              & yes & $\beta_{np}$, any sign \\
\code{"n"}    & normal              & no  & $\beta_{np}$, any sign \\
\code{"cln"}  & log-normal          & yes & $\exp(\beta_{np}) > 0$ \\
\code{"ln"}   & log-normal          & no  & $\exp(\beta_{np}) > 0$ \\
\code{"cln-"} & negative log-normal & yes & $-\exp(\beta_{np}) < 0$ \\
\code{"ln-"}  & negative log-normal & no  & $-\exp(\beta_{np}) < 0$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{The six mixing distributions of a random effect, their codes in the
argument \code{random\_effects}, and the coefficient of
(\ref{eq:mixing_map}) that enters the utility.}
\label{tab:mixing}
\end{table}

\subsection{Latent classes} \label{sec:model:classes}

A single normal distribution is a restrictive assumption about how preferences
are distributed over a population \citep[Section~6.2]{Train2009}. \pkg{RprobitB}
follows \citet{Oelschlaeger2021} and replaces the normal distribution in
(\ref{eq:random}) by a mixture of $C$ normal distributions,
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:mixture}
  \beta_n \sim \sum_{c = 1}^{C} s_c\, \mathrm{N}_{P_r}(b_c, \Omega_c),
\end{equation}
%
with class weights $s = (s_1, \dots, s_C)$ satisfying $s_c > 0$ and
$\sum_c s_c = 1$, and class-specific means $b_c$ and covariances $\Omega_c$. The
mixture is equivalent to a latent class model. An allocation variable
$z_n \in \{1, \dots, C\}$ assigns decider $n$ to class $c$ with probability
$\Prob(z_n = c) = s_c$, and conditional on the allocation, $\beta_n \sim
\mathrm{N}_{P_r}(b_{z_n}, \Omega_{z_n})$. Because the allocations are parameters
with a posterior distribution, the model classifies deciders by their
preferences \citep{Oelschlaeger2026c}. The argument \code{latent_class_effects}
of \fct{fit} names the effects that differ between the latent classes. A random
effect named there follows (\ref{eq:mixture}), whereas a random effect
not named there keeps one mean and one covariance for all classes and is
uncorrelated with the effects named there. A nonrandom
effect named there takes one value per class and does not vary within a class.
With the $P_l$ effects of the latter kind collected in
$\lambda_c \in \mathbb{R}^{P_l}$, the complete coefficient vector of decider $n$
is
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:partition}
  \theta_n = \big(\alpha^\top, \lambda_{z_n}^\top, g(\beta_n)^\top\big)^\top,
  \qquad P = P_f + P_l + P_r.
\end{equation}
%
If $P_l > 0$ but $P_r = 0$, (\ref{eq:partition}) is the classical latent class
model, in which all heterogeneity is due to the class membership
\citep{Kamakura1989, Greene2003}. If instead $P_r > 0$, it is the latent class
mixed probit model of \citet{Oelschlaeger2021}. The number of classes is either
fixed by the argument \code{classes} of \fct{fit}, or the number of occupied
classes is inferred from the data. The argument \code{class_update} selects
between a finite mixture whose classes all stay occupied (\code{"fixed"}), an
overfitted finite mixture with a sparse prior on the weights that empties
superfluous classes (\code{"sparse"};
\citealp{Rousseau2011, MalsinerWalli2016, FruehwirthSchnatter2019}), a Dirichlet
process mixture whose number of occupied classes is random
(\code{"dirichlet_process"}; \citealp{Escobar1995, Neal2000}), and the
weight-based remove, split, and merge heuristic of \citet{Oelschlaeger2021}
(\code{"weight_based"}). Section~\ref{sec:estimation} describes the
corresponding sampler steps, and Section~\ref{sec:latent_classes} applies all
four updating schemes to empirical and simulated data.

\subsection{Ordered and ranked responses} \label{sec:model:ordered_ranked}

Some responses are ordered levels, such as the agreement with a statement on a
Likert scale, for which one utility per level is not natural
\citep[Section~7.4]{Train2009}. Instead, the model has a single utility
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:ordered_utility}
  U_{nt} = \theta_n^0 + X_{nt}^\top \theta_n + \varepsilon_{nt}, \qquad
  \varepsilon_{nt} \sim \mathrm{N}(0, \sigma^2).
\end{equation}
%
The utility falls into one of $J$ intervals that are linked to the ordered
levels $j = 1, \dots, J$,
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:ordered_link}
  y_{nt} = j \quad \text{if and only if} \quad \gamma_{j - 1} < U_{nt} \leq
  \gamma_{j},
\end{equation}
%
with thresholds $\gamma_0 = -\infty < \gamma_1 < \dots < \gamma_{J - 1} <
\gamma_J = +\infty$. The thresholds are parameterized through logarithmic
increments $d \in \mathbb{R}^{J - 2}$ with $\gamma_j = \sum_{i = 1}^{j - 1}
\exp(d_i)$ for $j = 2, \dots, J - 1$. The level is normalized by $\gamma_1 =
0$ and the scale by $\sigma^2 = 1$. Random coefficients and latent classes apply
to (\ref{eq:ordered_utility}) exactly as to (\ref{eq:utility}).

Some surveys record a complete ranking of the alternatives, which is more
informative about the preferences \citep[Section~7.3]{Train2009}, as we
demonstrate in Section~\ref{sec:ranked}. The utilities of
(\ref{eq:utility}) stay the same, and only the link changes. The response
at occasion $t$ is a permutation $r_{nt} = (r_{nt1}, \dots, r_{ntJ})$ of the
alternatives, ordered from best to worst, and the model requires
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:ranked_link}
  U_{nt r_{nt1}} > U_{nt r_{nt2}} > \dots > U_{nt r_{ntJ}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Choice probabilities and likelihood} \label{sec:model:probabilities}

Conditional on $\theta_n$ and $\tilde{\Sigma}$, the utility differences of an
unordered choice are distributed as $\mathrm{N}_{J-1}(\tilde{X}_{nt} \theta_n,
\tilde{\Sigma})$, and the choice rule of (\ref{eq:link}) yields the
choice probability
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:choice_probability}
  \Prob(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, \theta_n, \tilde{\Sigma}) =
  \begin{cases}
    \Prob\big(\tilde{U}_{ntj} > 0,\ \tilde{U}_{ntj} > \tilde{U}_{ntl} \
    \text{for all } l \notin \{j, k\}\big) & \text{if } j \neq k, \\
    \Prob\big(\tilde{U}_{ntl} < 0 \ \text{for all } l \neq k\big) & \text{if } j
    = k,
  \end{cases}
\end{equation}
%
where $k$ is the base alternative of Section~\ref{sec:model:normalization}.
With individual choice sets, the inequalities run over the available
alternatives only. For a ranked
response, the probability of the ranking $r_{nt}$ is the probability that all $J
- 1$ utility differences between consecutively ranked alternatives in
(\ref{eq:ranked_link}) are positive. For an ordered response, the
probability of level $j$ follows from (\ref{eq:ordered_link}),
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:ordered_probability}
  \Prob(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, \theta_n, \gamma, \sigma^2) =
    \Phi\Big(\frac{\gamma_j - \theta_n^0 - X_{nt}^\top \theta_n}{\sigma}\Big) -
  \Phi\Big(\frac{\gamma_{j - 1} - \theta_n^0 - X_{nt}^\top
  \theta_n}{\sigma}\Big),
\end{equation}
%
with the standard normal distribution function $\Phi$.

The population parameters of a model are those without a decider index: the
fixed coefficients $\alpha$, the class coefficients $\lambda_c$, the means $b_c$
and covariances $\Omega_c$ of the random coefficients, the class weights $s$,
the error covariance $\tilde{\Sigma}$ or the error variance $\sigma^2$, and the
thresholds $\gamma$. We collect them in the vector $\vartheta$. The random
coefficients $\beta_n$ and the allocations $z_n$ are decider-level parameters,
and the likelihood contribution of a decider integrates them out. Let
$\Prob(y_{nt} \mid X_{nt}, \theta_n, \tilde{\Sigma})$ denote the probability of
the observed response $y_{nt}$, which is (\ref{eq:choice_probability}),
its ranked analog, or (\ref{eq:ordered_probability}) with $\gamma$ and
$\sigma^2$ in place of $\tilde{\Sigma}$. With $y_n = (y_{n1},
\dots, y_{nT_n})$ and $X_n$ the responses and covariates of decider $n$, the
likelihood of the decider is
%
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:likelihood}
  p(y_n \mid X_n, \vartheta) = \sum_{c = 1}^{C} s_c \int \prod_{t = 1}^{T_n}
  \Prob\big(y_{nt} \mid X_{nt}, \theta_n, \tilde{\Sigma}\big)\,
  \phi(\beta_n \mid b_c, \Omega_c)\, \mathrm{d}\beta_n,
\end{equation}
%
where $\theta_n = (\alpha^\top, \lambda_c^\top, g(\beta_n)^\top)^\top$ is the
coefficient vector of (\ref{eq:partition}) for a member of class $c$, and
$\phi(\cdot \mid b_c, \Omega_c)$ is the density of $\mathrm{N}_{P_r}(b_c,
\Omega_c)$. The likelihood of the sample $y = (y_1, \dots, y_N)$ is the product
of (\ref{eq:likelihood}) over all deciders, since the deciders are
assumed to be independent given the parameters.

\section[Bayesian estimation with fit()]{Bayesian estimation with \fct{fit}}
\label{sec:estimation}

\pkg{RprobitB} estimates every model of Section~\ref{sec:model} with a Gibbs
sampler. The sampler builds on \citet{McCulloch1994} and \citet{Imai2005a} for
the multinomial probit model, on \citet{Albert1993} for the ordered probit
model, and on \citet{Oelschlaeger2021} for the latent class mixed probit model.
It relies on data augmentation \citep{Albert1993}, which treats the latent
utilities as additional parameters. Conditional on the parameters, the utilities
follow truncated normal distributions whose truncation points are determined by
the observed responses. The sampler is written in \proglang{C++} for fast
computation and linked to \proglang{R} through \pkg{Rcpp}
\citep{Eddelbuettel2011} and \pkg{RcppArmadillo} \citep{Eddelbuettel2014}. In
this section, we introduce \fct{fit}, describe the prior distributions and the
sampler, and fit models to simulated data and to the choices of train travelers
in a stated choice experiment.

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Purpose & Arguments of \fct{fit} & Section \\
\hline
Model & \begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{formula}, \code{choice_type},
\code{alternatives}, \code{base}, \\ \code{scale}\end{tabular} &
\ref{sec:model:formula}, \ref{sec:model:normalization},
\ref{sec:model:ordered_ranked} \\
Heterogeneity & \begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{random_effects},
\code{latent_class_effects}, \code{classes}, \\ \code{class_update},
\code{max_classes}, \code{weight_based_control}\end{tabular} &
\ref{sec:model:random}, \ref{sec:model:classes}, \ref{sec:heterogeneity} \\
Data & \begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{data}, \code{format},
\code{column_decider}, \code{column_occasion}, \\ \code{column_alternative},
\code{delimiter}\end{tabular} & \ref{sec:fit_data} \\
Prior & \code{prior} & \ref{sec:est_prior} \\
Sampler & \begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{iterations}, \code{warmup},
\code{thin}, \code{chains}, \\ \code{save_individual_draws},
\code{progress}\end{tabular} & \ref{sec:est_gibbs}, \ref{sec:individual} \\
Simulation & \begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{n_deciders}, \code{n_occasions},
\code{n_alternatives}, \\ \code{covariates}, \code{dgp_parameters}\end{tabular}
& \ref{sec:fit_simulation} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Arguments of \fct{fit}, grouped by purpose, and the related sections.}
\label{tab:fit_arguments}
\end{table}

\subsection[The function fit()]{The function \fct{fit}} \label{sec:fit_data}

The function \fct{fit} takes a formula and a data frame, runs the Gibbs sampler,
and returns an object of class \class{RprobitB\_fit}, on which the methods
presented in the following sections operate.
Table~\ref{tab:fit_arguments} groups its arguments by their purpose. The help
page of \fct{fit}, accessible via \code{help(fit, package = "RprobitB")},
documents each of them.

\fct{fit} accepts a \class{data.frame} in the wide or in the long layout,
selected by the argument \code{format}. In the wide layout, every row is one
choice occasion, and a covariate that varies across alternatives occupies one
column per alternative, named by the covariate, the \code{delimiter}, and the
alternative, such as \code{price_bus} and \code{price_car}. In the long layout,
every row relates to one alternative of one choice occasion, and the argument
\code{column_alternative} names the column that identifies the alternative. An
occasion may then comprise only the rows of its available alternatives, which
specifies the individual choice sets of (\ref{eq:link}). The arguments
\code{column_decider} and \code{column_occasion} name the columns that identify
the decider and, in panel data, also the choice occasion.

By default, the sampler runs four chains of 1000 iterations each, half of them
warmup. With \code{data = NULL}, \fct{fit} first simulates a data set from the
specified model and then fits it (Section~\ref{sec:fit_simulation}). The
returned object holds, among other components, the data as a
\class{choice\_data} object of \pkg{choicedata}, the hyperparameters that were
used, and the retained draws of all chains as a \class{draws\_array} object of
\pkg{posterior}.

\subsection{Prior distributions} \label{sec:est_prior}

The priors are weakly informative by default and conjugate, which gives the
normal, inverse Wishart, and Dirichlet full conditional distributions of
Section~\ref{sec:est_gibbs}. Table~\ref{tab:est_prior} lists them with their
default hyperparameters and the names under which the argument \code{prior} of
\fct{fit} accepts replacements. The coefficients $\alpha$ and $\lambda_c$ and
the class means $b_c$ have normal priors centered at zero with variance 10. The
covariance matrices $\Omega_c$ of the random coefficients and
$\tilde{\Sigma}^\ast$ of the errors have inverse Wishart priors with $\nu$ and
$\kappa$ degrees of freedom and scale matrices $\Theta$ and $\Lambda$. As in
\citet{Imai2005a}, the prior of the error covariance is placed on the matrix
$\tilde{\Sigma}^\ast$ before the scale normalization, with $\tilde{\Sigma} =
\tilde{\Sigma}^\ast / \tilde{\Sigma}^\ast_{11}$ under the default normalization.
The priors of $\lambda_c$, $b_c$, and $\Omega_c$ are independent over the
classes. The class weights $s$ of a finite mixture have a symmetric Dirichlet
prior with concentration parameter $\delta$, which by default is fixed at one
for \code{class_update = "fixed"} and \code{"weight_based"}. For the sparse
finite mixture, $\delta$ receives a gamma hyperprior with shape $a_\delta$ and
rate $b_\delta$, by default $(a_\delta, b_\delta) = (1, 200)$ as in
\citet{FruehwirthSchnatter2019}. Under the Dirichlet process, $\delta$ is the
concentration parameter of the process, with the hyperprior $(a_\delta,
b_\delta) = (2, 4)$ of \citet{Escobar1995}. The logarithmic threshold increments
$d$ of an ordered model have a standard normal prior.

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\hline
Parameter & Prior & Default & Components of \code{prior} \\
\hline
$\alpha$ & $\mathrm{N}_{P_f}(\psi, \Psi)$ & $\psi = 0$, $\Psi = 10 I_{P_f}$ &
\begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{fixed_mean} \\
\code{fixed_covariance}\end{tabular} \\
$\lambda_c$ & $\mathrm{N}_{P_l}(\psi_\lambda, \Psi_\lambda)$ & $\psi_\lambda =
0$, $\Psi_\lambda = 10 I_{P_l}$ &
\begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{latent_class_mean} \\
\code{latent_class_covariance}\end{tabular} \\
$b_c$ & $\mathrm{N}_{P_r}(\xi, \Xi)$ & $\xi = 0$, $\Xi = 10 I_{P_r}$ &
\begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{random_mean} \\
\code{random_mean_covariance}\end{tabular} \\
$\Omega_c$ & $\mathrm{W}^{-1}_{P_r}(\nu, \Theta)$ &
$\nu = P_r + 2$, $\Theta = I_{P_r}$ &
\begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{random_covariance_df} \\
\code{random_covariance_scale}\end{tabular} \\
$s$ & $\mathrm{Dir}_C(\delta, \dots, \delta)$ & $\delta = 1$ &
\code{class_concentration} \\
$\delta$ & $\mathrm{Gamma}(a_\delta, b_\delta)$ &
$(a_\delta, b_\delta) = (1, 200)$ or $(2, 4)$ &
\code{class_concentration} \\
$\tilde{\Sigma}^\ast$ & $\mathrm{W}^{-1}_{J-1}(\kappa, \Lambda)$ &
$\kappa = J + 1$,
$\Lambda = I_{J-1}$ & \begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{error_covariance_df} \\
\code{error_covariance_scale}\end{tabular} \\
$d$ & $\mathrm{N}_{J-2}(\zeta, \Upsilon)$ &
$\zeta = 0$, $\Upsilon = I_{J-2}$ &
\begin{tabular}[t]{@{}l@{}}\code{threshold_mean} \\
\code{threshold_covariance}\end{tabular} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Prior distributions, their default hyperparameters, and the
corresponding components of the argument \code{prior} of \fct{fit}. The
Dirichlet prior of $s$ applies to finite mixtures; under the Dirichlet process,
$\delta$ is the concentration parameter of the process.}
\label{tab:est_prior}
\end{table}

\subsection{The Gibbs sampler} \label{sec:est_gibbs}

For unordered and ranked responses, the latent variables that augment the
data are the utility differences $\tilde{U}_{nt} \in \mathbb{R}^{J-1}$ of
(\ref{eq:utility_diff}). Their systematic part is
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:est_systematic}
\mu_{nt} = \tilde{W}_{nt}\alpha + \tilde{L}_{nt}\lambda_{z_n} +
\tilde{V}_{nt}\,g(\beta_n),
\end{equation}
%
where $\tilde{W}_{nt}$, $\tilde{L}_{nt}$, and $\tilde{V}_{nt}$ are the columns
of the differenced covariates that belong to $\alpha$, $\lambda_c$, and
$\beta_n$, and the error of $\tilde{U}_{nt}$ is $\tilde{\varepsilon}_{nt} \sim
\mathrm{N}_{J-1}(0, \tilde{\Sigma})$. Given the utilities and $\tilde{\Sigma}$,
the model is a linear regression. Every coefficient block
$\rho \in \mathbb{R}^{P_\rho}$ with prior $\mathrm{N}_{P_\rho}(\rho_0, V_0)$ and
design matrices $Z_{nt}$ hence has the normal full conditional distribution
%
\begin{align}
\rho \mid \cdot &\sim \mathrm{N}_{P_\rho}\big(\Sigma_\rho\, \mu_\rho,
\Sigma_\rho\big), \label{eq:est_regression} \\
\mu_\rho &= V_0^{-1}\rho_0 + \sum_{n, t} Z_{nt}^\top \tilde{\Sigma}^{-1}
(\tilde{U}_{nt} - q_{nt}), \qquad
\Sigma_\rho = \Big(V_0^{-1} + \sum_{n, t} Z_{nt}^\top \tilde{\Sigma}^{-1}
Z_{nt}\Big)^{-1}, \nonumber
\end{align}
%
where $q_{nt}$ is the part of $\mu_{nt}$ that does not depend on $\rho$. Every
chain starts at a draw from the prior distributions of the coefficients and
thresholds, with the random coefficients at their class means, $\Omega_c$ at the
prior scale $\Theta$, $\tilde{\Sigma}$ at the identity, the deciders
allocated to the classes at random, and the utilities at zero, so that the
chains start dispersed. One iteration then updates the following blocks in
turn:
%
\begin{enumerate}
\item The class weights $s$ of a finite mixture are drawn from
$\mathrm{Dir}_C(\delta + m_1, \dots, \delta + m_C)$, where $m_c$ is the number
of deciders in class $c$. Under the Dirichlet process, the weights are
integrated out by the algorithm of \citet{Neal2000}, and the package reports the
class shares $m_c / N$.
\item For every class $c$, the mean $b_c$ and the covariance $\Omega_c$ follow
the conjugate normal and inverse Wishart updates given the sample $\{\beta_n :
z_n = c\}$.
\item The class coefficients $\lambda_c$ follow
(\ref{eq:est_regression}) with $Z_{nt} = \tilde{L}_{nt}$ and the sums
over the deciders of class $c$.
\item For every decider $n$, the random coefficients $\beta_n$ follow
(\ref{eq:est_regression}) with $Z_{nt} = \tilde{V}_{nt}$, the sums over
the occasions of decider $n$, and the class distribution
$\mathrm{N}_{P_r}(b_{z_n}, \Omega_{z_n})$ as the prior. With a log-normal random
effect, an elliptical slice sampling step \citep{Murray2010} replaces this
update. The fixed coefficients $\alpha$ then follow (\ref{eq:est_regression})
with $Z_{nt} = \tilde{W}_{nt}$ and the sums over all deciders.
\item Decider $n$ is allocated to class $c$ with probability
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:est_allocation}
\Prob(z_n = c \mid \cdot) \propto s_c\, \phi(\beta_n \mid b_c, \Omega_c)
\prod_{t = 1}^{T_n} \Prob(y_{nt} \mid X_{nt}, \alpha, \lambda_c, \beta_n,
\tilde{\Sigma}).
\end{equation}
%
The four values of \code{class_update} differ in the treatment of the number of
classes. For \code{"fixed"}, the last member of a class stays in it, which keeps
all $C$ classes occupied. For \code{"sparse"}, $C$ is an upper bound, and the
small concentration parameter empties the classes that the data do not require.
For \code{"dirichlet_process"}, deciders open new classes, up to
\code{max_classes}, by Algorithm~8 of \citet{Neal2000}. For
\code{"weight_based"}, the heuristic of \citet{Oelschlaeger2021} removes,
splits, or merges classes during the warmup.
\item In an ordered model, the thresholds $\gamma$ are updated by a random-walk
Metropolis--Hastings step on their logarithmic increments $d$.
\item Given the parameters, the utility differences $\tilde{U}_{nt}$ of an
unordered choice are $\mathrm{N}_{J-1}(\mu_{nt}, \tilde{\Sigma})$ truncated to
the region in which the chosen alternative has the largest utility, and the
truncated normal draws use the samplers of \citet{Robert1995}. For a ranked
response, the truncation is chosen such that the observed ranking holds. An
ordered response has a single utility $U_{nt} = \mu_{nt} + \varepsilon_{nt}$
with $\varepsilon_{nt} \sim \mathrm{N}(0, 1)$, drawn from
$\mathrm{N}(\mu_{nt}, 1)$ truncated to $(\gamma_{y_{nt} - 1}, \gamma_{y_{nt}}]$.
\item The error covariance is drawn from $\tilde{\Sigma}^\ast \sim
\mathrm{W}^{-1}_{J-1}(\kappa + \sum_n T_n, \Lambda + S)$ with the scatter matrix
$S$ of the residuals $\tilde{U}_{nt} - \mu_{nt}$ and normalized to
$\tilde{\Sigma} = \tilde{\Sigma}^\ast / \tilde{\Sigma}^\ast_{11}$.
\end{enumerate}

Of the \code{iterations} of every chain, the first \code{warmup} are discarded
and every \code{thin}-th of the remaining ones is retained. The $D$ retained
draws of all chains are rescaled to the normalization requested by \code{scale}.
In a mixture model, a chain may swap the class labels during a run
\citep{Stephens2000}. The draws are therefore relabeled after sampling by
matching them to a reference partition \citep{Dahl2006, Papastamoulis2010}, and
the classes are numbered by decreasing posterior mean weight.

\subsection{Parameter recovery with simulated data} \label{sec:fit_simulation}

With \code{data = NULL}, \fct{fit} simulates the choices from the specified
model before it fits the model, which can be used to verify that the sampler
recovers the parameters of a known data generating process (DGP). As a
demonstration example below, we simulate 400 deciders who choose 20 times among
bus, car, and train and who belong to one of three latent classes. The formula
contains the three covariate types of Section~\ref{sec:model:formula}: the price
with one coefficient for all alternatives, which we specify as a normal random
effect whose mean and variance differ between the classes, the income of the
decider with one coefficient per alternative, and the travel time, whose values
and coefficients both differ between the alternatives. We collect the parameters
of the DGP in a list containing, for each class, the coefficients \code{beta},
whose entry \code{price} is the mean of the random price coefficient, and the
variance \code{Omega} of this coefficient, as well as the class \code{weights}
and the covariance matrix \code{Sigma} of the three errors. Covariates that are
not supplied through the argument \code{covariates} are drawn from a standard
normal distribution.
%
<<simulation-dgp>>=
common <- c(
  income_car = 0.5, income_train = -0.5, ASC_car = 1, ASC_train = 0.5,
  time_bus = -0.5, time_car = -1, time_train = -1.5
)
dgp_parameters <- list(
  beta = list(
    c(price = -1.75, common), c(price = 0, common),
    c(price = 1.75, common)
  ),
  Omega = list(matrix(0.05), matrix(0.05), matrix(0.05)),
  weights = c(1, 1, 1) / 3, Sigma = diag(c(0.5, 0.5, 1))
)
@
%
\fct{fit} simulates the data from these parameters and fits the model:
%
<<simulation>>=
simulation <- fit(
  choice ~ price | income | time, random_effects = "price",
  latent_class_effects = "price", classes = 3,
  alternatives = c("bus", "car", "train"), base = "bus",
  n_deciders = 400, n_occasions = 20, dgp_parameters = dgp_parameters,
  iterations = 40000, warmup = 20000, thin = 20, chains = 2
)
@
%
\fct{model.frame} returns the simulated data in the wide layout:
%
<<simulation-frame>>=
head(model.frame(simulation), 3)
@
%
\fct{summary} lists the parameters of the DGP in the column \code{dgp}, in the
normalization of the estimates. The entries of \code{Sigma} therefore refer to
the utility differences with respect to bus, the base alternative, with the
variance of the first difference fixed at one
(Section~\ref{sec:model:normalization}):
%
<<simulation-summary>>=
summary(simulation)
@
%
\fct{summary} also assesses the draws with the diagnostics of
\citet{Vehtari2021}. The column \code{rhat} is the potential scale reduction
factor $\hat{R}$, which compares the variation between and within the halves of
all chains. It exceeds one if the half-chains differ in location or spread,
which indicates that the chains have not converged, and should be at most
$1.01$. The column \code{ess_bulk} is the bulk effective sample size, the number
of independent draws that estimate the center of the posterior as precisely as
the retained draws, and should be at least $100$ per chain. Both diagnostics are
within these bounds, and the posterior means are close to the parameters of the
DGP.

\subsection{A stated choice experiment on train trips} \label{sec:fit_train}

In 1987, a survey commissioned by the Dutch national railways presented
travelers with pairs of hypothetical train trips and asked which one they would
book \citep{BenAkiva1993}. The two trips of a pair can differ in price, travel
time, number of changes, and comfort class. The \code{Train} data are provided
by the \pkg{mlogit} package. The prices are recorded in cents of Dutch guilders
and the travel times in minutes. We convert the prices to euro at the fixed
conversion rate of 2.20371 guilders per euro and the times to hours.
%
<<train-data>>=
data("Train", package = "mlogit")
Train$price_A <- Train$price_A / 100 / 2.20371
Train$price_B <- Train$price_B / 100 / 2.20371
Train$time_A <- Train$time_A / 60
Train$time_B <- Train$time_B / 60
@
%
The data contain \Sexpr{nrow(Train)} choices of \Sexpr{length(unique(Train$id))}
travelers, between \Sexpr{min(table(Train$id))} and \Sexpr{max(table(Train$id))}
per traveler. The comfort class is coded as a number from \code{0}, the best
class, to \code{2}, the worst, and \code{factor(comfort)} converts it into two
dummy variables relative to the best class.
%
<<train-fit>>=
model_train <- fit(
  choice ~ price + time + change + factor(comfort) | 0,
  data = Train, column_decider = "id", column_occasion = "choiceid",
  iterations = 2000, warmup = 1000, chains = 2
)
summary(model_train)
@
%
The \fct{plot} method draws trace, rank, autocorrelation, density, interval, or
pairs plots of the retained draws with \pkg{bayesplot}. Figure~\ref{fig:trace}
shows the trace plots of \code{model_train}, in which the chains fluctuate
around a constant level and occupy the same range.
%
<<train-trace, fig=TRUE, width=9, height=2.5>>=
plot(model_train, type = "trace")
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{train-trace}
\caption{Trace plots of the two chains of \code{model\_train}.}
\label{fig:trace}
\end{figure}

\section{Interpreting the posterior} \label{sec:interpretation}

We summarize the posterior of the train model and derive compensations and
marginal effects.

\fct{coef} returns the posterior mean of every population parameter as a point
estimate:
%
<<train-coef>>=
coef(model_train)
@
%
\fct{confint} returns equal-tailed credible intervals, whose probability is set
by \code{level}:
%
<<train-confint>>=
confint(model_train, level = 0.9)
@

\subsection{Compensations} \label{sec:compensations}

The values of the coefficients depend on the scale normalization of
Section~\ref{sec:model:normalization}, whereas their ratios are invariant to it.
Because the utility is linear in the covariates, an increase of one unit in the
covariate of effect $p$ leaves the utility unchanged if the covariate of effect
$q$ changes by
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:compensation}
\tau_{pq} = -\frac{\theta_p}{\theta_q}
\end{equation}
%
units \citep{Train2009}, the compensation of effect $p$ in units of effect $q$.
With the price as reference effect $q$, $\tau_{pq}$ is the willingness to pay
for one unit of effect $p$: the price increase that leaves the utility of the
alternative unchanged, which is negative for an undesired effect.
\fct{interpret} evaluates (\ref{eq:compensation}) under every draw and
summarizes the posterior of each ratio. For \code{model\_train}, the
compensations with the price as reference are in euro at 1987 prices:
%
<<train-compensation>>=
train_compensation <- interpret(model_train, reference = "price")
train_compensation
@
%
A traveler is willing to pay about 5.4 euro to save one hour of travel time
(95\% credible interval from 4.5 to 6.3 euro), about 1.0 euro to avoid a change
of trains, and about 6.8 euro for the best instead of the worst comfort class.

\subsection{Marginal effects}

The choice probabilities depend on the utilities through the nonlinear probit
link. The partial derivative of a probability with respect to a covariate, its
marginal effect, therefore varies with the covariate values. \fct{interpret}
computes marginal effects by numerical differentiation. Let $\pi_j(X_{nt},
\vartheta) = \Prob(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, \vartheta)$ denote the probability of
alternative $j$ at occasion $t$ of decider $n$, and let $X_{nt}^{+h}$ and
$X_{nt}^{-h}$ be $X_{nt}$ with a numeric covariate $x$ of alternative $j$
shifted by $+h$ and $-h$. The marginal effect of $x$ on $\pi_j$ under draw $d$
is the central difference
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:marginal_effect}
\mathrm{ME}_{ntj}^{(d)}(x) = \frac{\pi_j\big(X_{nt}^{+h}, \vartheta^{(d)}\big) -
\pi_j\big(X_{nt}^{-h}, \vartheta^{(d)}\big)}{2h},
\end{equation}
%
where $h$ is a small step size; effects of $x$ on the probabilities of the other
alternatives are not computed. The marginal effect at the average (MEA,
\code{type = "mea"}) evaluates (\ref{eq:marginal_effect}) at one artificial
occasion whose covariates are the averages or, for factors, the modes of the
observed ones, and the average marginal effect (AME, \code{type = "ame"})
averages it over all observed choice occasions:
%
<<train-mea>>=
train_mea <- interpret(model_train, type = "mea")
print(as.data.frame(train_mea), digits = 3, row.names = FALSE)
@
%
At the average occasion, where the two trips are nearly identical in terms of
their attributes, one euro more on a trip lowers the probability that the trip
is chosen by about 8 percentage points, one hour more by about 42 points, and
one more change of trains by about 8 points. The argument \code{at} replaces
selected averages by chosen values. With \code{at = c(price_A = 20, price_B =
10)}, trip \code{B} is chosen with a probability close to one, and one euro then
changes the probability by only about 1.1 percentage points.
Figure~\ref{fig:fare_curve} illustrates this nonlinearity.
%
<<fare-curve-figure, fig=TRUE, echo=FALSE, width=6, height=2.1>>=
train_mea_at <- interpret(
  model_train, type = "mea", at = c(price_A = 20, price_B = 10)
)
average_occasion <- with(
  as.data.frame(train_mea),
  setNames(at, paste(covariate, alternative, sep = "_"))
)
fare_difference <- seq(-15, 15, by = 0.5)
fare_grid <- data.frame(
  id = seq_along(fare_difference), choiceid = 1,
  price_A = average_occasion[["price_A"]],
  price_B = average_occasion[["price_A"]] + fare_difference,
  time_A = average_occasion[["time_A"]],
  time_B = average_occasion[["time_B"]],
  change_A = average_occasion[["change_A"]],
  change_B = average_occasion[["change_B"]],
  comfort_A = 1, comfort_B = 1
)
fare_curve <- predict(model_train, newdata = fare_grid)
fare_points <- c(
  average_occasion[["price_B"]] - average_occasion[["price_A"]], -10
)
fare_slopes <- c(
  subset(train_mea, covariate == "price" & alternative == "B")$mean,
  subset(train_mea_at, covariate == "price" & alternative == "B")$mean
)
fare_probability <- approx(
  fare_difference, fare_curve$probability_B, xout = fare_points
)$y
op <- par(mar = c(4, 4, 0.5, 0.5))
plot(
  fare_difference, fare_curve$probability_B, type = "l", lwd = 3,
  col = "gray60", ylim = c(0, 1), yaxt = "n",
  xlab = "price of trip B minus price of trip A in euro",
  ylab = "probability of trip B"
)
axis(2, at = c(0, 0.5, 1))
segments(
  fare_points - 4, fare_probability - 4 * fare_slopes,
  fare_points + 4, fare_probability + 4 * fare_slopes, lty = 2, lwd = 2
)
points(fare_points, fare_probability, pch = 19)
par(op)
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics{fare-curve-figure}
\caption{Posterior predictive probability of trip \code{B} as a function of the
price difference, with all other covariates at the values of the average
occasion. The points mark the average occasion and the occasion at which trip
\code{B} costs 10 euro and trip \code{A} 20 euro, and the dashed lines have the
slopes of the posterior mean marginal effects of the price at these occasions.}
\label{fig:fare_curve}
\end{figure}
%
\fct{interpret} with \code{type = "ame"} returns the AMEs:
%
<<train-ame>>=
train_ame <- interpret(model_train, type = "ame")
print(as.data.frame(train_ame), digits = 3, row.names = FALSE)
@
%
Averaged over the observed pairs of trips, one euro more lowers the probability
of a trip by about 6.5 percentage points and one hour more by about 35 points.

\section{Modeling preference heterogeneity} \label{sec:heterogeneity}

We let the coefficients vary between deciders through random coefficients and
latent classes. Both benefit from panel data, whose repeated choices separate
this variation from the errors.

\subsection{Random coefficients} \label{sec:random_coefficients}

The \code{Electricity} data of \pkg{mlogit} come from a stated choice experiment
in which 361 households in the United States chose 8 to 12 times among four
hypothetical suppliers \citep{Huber2001}. The suppliers differ in the price
\code{pf} in cents per kilowatt hour (kWh), the contract length \code{cl} in
years, whether the supplier is local (\code{loc}) or well-known (\code{wk}), and
whether it offers time-of-day (\code{tod}) or seasonal (\code{seas}) rates. We
insert an underscore into the column names and then number the choice occasions
of every household:
%
<<electricity-data>>=
data("Electricity", package = "mlogit")
names(Electricity) <- sub("([1-4])$", "_\\1", names(Electricity))
Electricity$occasion <- with(Electricity, ave(id, id, FUN = seq_along))
@
%
Contract length and locality receive correlated normal random coefficients.
Fixing the price coefficient at $-1$ expresses every other coefficient in cents
per kWh, that is, as a willingness to pay. We save the individual draws, from
which Section~\ref{sec:individual} derives the coefficients of single
households.
%
<<electricity-fit>>=
electricity <- fit(
  choice ~ pf + cl + loc + wk + tod + seas | 0, data = Electricity,
  random_effects = c("cl", "loc"), column_decider = "id",
  column_occasion = "occasion", scale = c(pf = -1),
  iterations = 25000, warmup = 10000, thin = 20, chains = 2,
  save_individual_draws = TRUE
)
summary(electricity)
@
%
The signs of \code{mu[cl]}, \code{mu[loc]}, and \code{beta[wk]} show that
households on average dislike long contracts and prefer local and well-known
suppliers. A household at the population mean accepts a price about
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", coef(electricity)[["mu[loc]"]])} cents per kWh higher for
a local supplier and about
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", coef(electricity)[["beta[wk]"]])} cents higher for a
well-known supplier, whereas each additional year of contract length has to be
compensated by a price about
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", abs(coef(electricity)[["mu[cl]"]]))} cents lower. The
large variance \code{Omega[loc,loc]} shows that households differ considerably
in their valuation of a local supplier.

\subsection{Individual deciders} \label{sec:individual}

A mixed model fitted with \code{save_individual_draws = TRUE} retains the
posterior draws of every decider's coefficients. Conditional on the population
parameters, the posterior of the random coefficient vector $\beta_n$ of decider
$n$ combines the population distribution of (\ref{eq:random}) with the
decider's own choices,
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:individual_posterior}
p(\beta_n \mid y, \vartheta) \propto \phi(\beta_n \mid b, \Omega) \prod_{t =
1}^{T_n} \Prob(y_{nt} \mid X_{nt}, \theta_n, \tilde{\Sigma}).
\end{equation}
%
Equation~(\ref{eq:individual_posterior}) is the conditional distribution of the
coefficients given the decider's choices \citep[Chapter~11]{Train2009}, in
contrast to the unconditional population distribution of (\ref{eq:random}).
\code{coef(level = "individual")} returns the posterior means of the individual
coefficients, one row per household:
%
<<household-coefficients>>=
household_coefficients <- coef(electricity, level = "individual")
head(household_coefficients, 5)
@
%
Because the price coefficient is fixed at $-1$, the \code{loc} column holds each
household's willingness to pay for a local supplier in cents per kWh. Its first
and ninth deciles show how widely the posterior mean valuations spread around
the population mean \code{mu[loc]} of
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", coef(electricity)[["mu[loc]"]])} cents:
%
<<household-deciles>>=
quantile(household_coefficients[, "loc"], probs = c(0.1, 0.9))
@
%
A tenth of the households thus have a posterior mean valuation below
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", quantile(household_coefficients[, "loc"], 0.1))} cents,
and another tenth above
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", quantile(household_coefficients[, "loc"], 0.9))} cents.

\subsection{Mixing distributions} \label{sec:mixing_distributions}

The mixing distribution determines the support and the shape of the preference
heterogeneity in the population. A normal distribution admits coefficients of
both signs, whereas economic theory may restrict a coefficient, such as that of
the price, to one sign, which a log-normal distribution enforces. We illustrate
this with the train data of Section~\ref{sec:fit_train} and make the price
coefficient a random effect, first with a normal and then with a negative
log-normal mixing distribution. \fct{update} refits \code{model\_train} with the
named arguments replaced.
%
<<price-normal>>=
price_normal <- update(
  model_train, random_effects = c(price = "n"),
  iterations = 6000, warmup = 3000, thin = 30
)
@
%
<<price-lognormal>>=
price_lognormal <- update(
  model_train, random_effects = c(price = "ln-"),
  iterations = 6000, warmup = 3000, thin = 30
)
@
%
With a normal mixing distribution, the population density places
10 percent of its mass on positive price
coefficients, whereas the negative log-normal mixing distribution excludes
them. Figure~\ref{fig:price_mixing} shows the two population densities of the
price coefficient, the densities of (\ref{eq:random}) averaged over the
retained draws of the population parameters. The \fct{plot} method draws them
with \code{type = "mixture"}.
%
<<price-mixing-figure, fig=TRUE, echo=FALSE, width=6, height=2.1>>=
normal <- plot(price_normal, type = "mixture")$data
lognormal <- plot(price_lognormal, type = "mixture")$data
op <- par(mar = c(4, 4, 0.5, 0.5))
plot(
  normal$value, normal$density, type = "l", lwd = 2,
  ylim = c(0, 3 * max(normal$density)),
  xlab = "price coefficient", ylab = "density"
)
lines(lognormal$value, lognormal$density, lwd = 2, lty = 2)
abline(v = 0, lty = 3)
legend(
  "topleft", legend = c("normal", "negative log-normal"),
  lwd = 2, lty = c(1, 2), bty = "n"
)
par(op)
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.56\textwidth]{price-mixing-figure}
\caption{Population density of the price coefficient under the normal mixing
distribution \code{"n"} (solid) and the negative log-normal mixing
distribution \code{"ln-"} (dashed).}
\label{fig:price_mixing}
\end{figure}
%
\fct{interpret} evaluates the compensation of a random coefficient at the
median of its mixing distribution. We compare the compensation of an hour of
travel time, with its 95\% credible interval, across the three models:
%
<<mixed-compensation>>=
models <- mget(c("model_train", "price_normal", "price_lognormal"))
times <- lapply(models, interpret, reference = "price", effects = "time")
value_of_time <- sapply(times, function(x) unlist(as.data.frame(x)[-1]))
print(value_of_time, digits = 3)
@
%
The value of time of the traveler with the median price coefficient is about
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", -value_of_time["mean", "price_normal"])}~euro under
the normal and about
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", -value_of_time["mean", "price_lognormal"])}~euro
under the negative log-normal mixing distribution, compared with
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", -value_of_time["mean", "model_train"])}~euro for the
fixed coefficient of \code{model\_train}. The choice of the mixing distribution
thus shifts the estimated value of time of this traveler by almost one euro. In
Section~\ref{sec:evaluation}, we compare the three models by their predictive
accuracy.

\subsection{Latent classes} \label{sec:latent_classes}

In the model of Section~\ref{sec:random_coefficients}, the valuation of a local
supplier follows a single normal distribution, which cannot represent skewed or
multimodal valuations. To examine whether the households form segments with
distinct valuations, we let this valuation follow a mixture of two normal
distributions with class-specific weights, means, and variances:
%
<<electricity-classes>>=
electricity_classes <- update(
  electricity, latent_class_effects = "loc", classes = 2
)
summary(electricity_classes)
@
%
The larger class, 82 percent of the households, values a local supplier at
about 1.6 cents per kWh, the smaller class at about 4.9 cents.
Figure~\ref{fig:electricity_classes} shows the mixing distribution of the
valuation under this model, the mixture of the two class-specific normal
distributions averaged over the retained draws, together with the posterior
mean valuations of the households.
%
<<electricity-classes-figure, fig=TRUE, echo=FALSE, width=6, height=2.7>>=
classes_draws <- posterior::as_draws_matrix(electricity_classes$draws)
class_grid <- seq(-4, 12, length.out = 400)
class_density <- sapply(1:2, function(k) {
  weight <- classes_draws[, sprintf("weight[%d]", k)]
  mean_k <- classes_draws[, sprintf("mu[loc,%d]", k)]
  sd_k <- sqrt(classes_draws[, sprintf("Omega[loc,loc,%d]", k)])
  rowMeans(sapply(seq_along(weight), function(d) {
    weight[d] * dnorm(class_grid, mean_k[d], sd_k[d])
  }))
})
op <- par(mar = c(4, 4, 0.5, 0.5))
hist(
  coef(electricity_classes, level = "individual")[, "loc"], breaks = 30,
  freq = FALSE, col = "gray85", border = "white", main = "",
  xlim = range(class_grid), ylab = "density",
  xlab = "valuation of a local supplier in cents per kWh"
)
lines(class_grid, rowSums(class_density), lwd = 2)
lines(class_grid, class_density[, 1], lty = 2)
lines(class_grid, class_density[, 2], lty = 3)
par(op)
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics{electricity-classes-figure}
\caption{Mixing distribution of the valuation of a local supplier in the
two-class model \code{electricity\_classes} (solid) and its weighted class
components (dashed for class 1, dotted for class 2). The histogram shows the
posterior mean valuations $\beta_{n,\mathrm{loc}}$ of the 361 households under
this model.}
\label{fig:electricity_classes}
\end{figure}

A Dirichlet process mixture makes the number of occupied classes part of the
posterior. Given its concentration $\delta$, the prior expected number of
occupied classes among $N$ deciders is $\sum_{i = 1}^{N} \delta / (\delta + i -
1)$ \citep{Escobar1995}, which gives about
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", sum(0.5 / (0.5 + seq_len(361) - 1)))} classes for $N =
361$ at the prior mean $\delta = 0.5$ of the default hyperprior:
%
<<dirichlet>>=
dynamic <- update(
  electricity_classes, class_update = "dirichlet_process"
)
summary(dynamic, variables = c(
  "weight[1]", "weight[2]", "weight[3]", "mu[loc,1]", "mu[loc,2]",
  "mu[loc,3]", "class_concentration", "n_classes"
))
@
%
Under the Dirichlet process, the number of occupied classes varies over the
draws, and the column \code{occupied} reports the share of draws in which a
class is occupied. The two classes of the finite mixture persist in every draw
with similar weights and valuations, whereas a third class is occupied in about
half of the draws. \fct{latent\_class\_diagnostics} summarizes this posterior
through the distribution of the number of occupied classes and the membership
probabilities of every household. The occupancy table places a posterior
probability of 47 percent on two classes and 53 percent on three or more:
%
<<diagnostics>>=
dynamic_diagnostics <- latent_class_diagnostics(dynamic)
subset(dynamic_diagnostics$occupancy, probability > 0)
head(dynamic_diagnostics$membership[, 1:3], 3)
@
%
A sparse finite mixture addresses the same question within a finite model. It
starts with more classes than needed, here \code{classes = 6}, and its default
hyperprior with mean $\delta = 0.005$ favors small class weights, which
empties the superfluous classes. The sparse mixture assigns a posterior
probability of 91 percent to two occupied classes:
%
<<sparse>>=
sparse <- update(
  electricity_classes, class_update = "sparse", classes = 6
)
subset(latent_class_diagnostics(sparse)$occupancy, probability > 0)
@
%
The fourth updating scheme is the heuristic of \citet{Oelschlaeger2021},
requested by \code{class_update = "weight_based"}. During the warmup, it removes
a class whose weight falls below \code{epsmin = 0.01}, splits a class whose
weight exceeds \code{epsmax = 0.7}, and merges the two classes whose means are
closer than \code{deltamin = 0.1}, at most one change per \code{buffer = 50}
iterations. These values are the defaults of \code{weight_based_control}. After
the warmup, the number of classes stays fixed. We demonstrate the heuristic on
the simulation of Section~\ref{sec:fit_simulation}, whose three classes are
known, and start it once with a single class and once with ten classes to check
whether the result depends on the initial number of classes:
%
<<heuristic>>=
heuristic_one <- update(
  simulation, class_update = "weight_based", classes = 1
)
heuristic_ten <- update(
  simulation, class_update = "weight_based", classes = 10
)
@
%
Figure~\ref{fig:heuristic_classes} traces the number of classes during the
warmup of both chains of the two runs. Started with one class, the heuristic
splits it twice within the first 100 iterations. Started with ten classes, it
mostly removes small classes and occasionally merges two. All four chains settle
at three classes within the first 800 iterations.
%
<<heuristic-classes, fig=TRUE, echo=FALSE, width=6, height=2.8>>=
heuristic_runs <- list(one = heuristic_one, ten = heuristic_ten)
iterations <- 1:1000
op <- par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 1.5, 0.5))
for (run in c("one", "ten")) {
  classes <- heuristic_runs[[run]]$model$latent_classes
  history <- classes$history[iterations, ]
  changes <- classes$changes[iterations, ]
  plot(
    iterations, history[, 1], type = "s", ylim = c(1, 10),
    xlab = "iteration", ylab = "number of classes",
    main = paste("start with", run, if (run == "one") "class" else "classes")
  )
  lines(iterations, history[, 2], type = "s", lty = 2)
  for (chain in 1:2) {
    at <- which(changes[, chain] > 0)
    points(
      at, history[at, chain], pch = c(25, 24, 21)[changes[at, chain]],
      bg = "white"
    )
  }
  if (run == "one") {
    legend(
      "topright", legend = c("removal", "split", "merge"),
      pch = c(25, 24, 21), pt.bg = "white", bty = "n"
    )
  } else {
    legend(
      "topright", legend = c("chain 1", "chain 2"), lty = 1:2, bty = "n"
    )
  }
}
par(op)
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics{heuristic-classes}
\caption{Number of classes during the first 1000 warmup
iterations under the weight-based heuristic, started with one class (left)
and with ten classes (right).}
\label{fig:heuristic_classes}
\end{figure}
%
The weights and means of the classes found by the run from ten classes are close
to those of the DGP, and the run from a single class yields a similar result:
%
<<heuristic-summary>>=
summary(heuristic_ten, variables = c(
  "weight[1]", "weight[2]", "weight[3]", "mu[price,1]", "mu[price,2]",
  "mu[price,3]", "n_classes"
))
@

\section{Ordered and ranked responses} \label{sec:ordered_ranked}

\fct{fit} estimates the models of Section~\ref{sec:model:ordered_ranked} with
\code{choice_type = "ordered"} or \code{"ranked"}.

\subsection{Ordered responses} \label{sec:ordered}

We model the self-reported health satisfaction of adults as a function of age,
income, the degree of handicap in percent (\code{handper}), and marital status,
and quantify its persistent person-specific component. The \code{Health} data of
the \pkg{Rchoice} package, an extract of the German Socio-Economic Panel
compiled by \citet{Riphahn2003}, comprise 27326 observations of 7293 persons
from seven survey years between 1984 and 1994. The response \code{newhsat}
records the satisfaction with health on five ordered levels, from 0 for the
lowest to 4 for the highest satisfaction, which correspond to $j = 1, \dots, 5$
in (\ref{eq:ordered_link}). A normal random effect on the intercept
$\theta_n^0$ captures the between-person variation of the baseline satisfaction.
Age and monthly household net income in thousand marks are centered at their
sample means.
%
<<ordered-fit>>=
data("Health", package = "Rchoice")
Health$age <- Health$age - mean(Health$age)
Health$income <- Health$hhinc / 1000 - mean(Health$hhinc / 1000)
Health$occasion <- with(Health, ave(id, id, FUN = seq_along))
satisfaction <- fit(
  newhsat ~ age + income + handper + married, data = Health,
  alternatives = as.character(0:4), choice_type = "ordered",
  random_effects = "ASC", column_decider = "id",
  column_occasion = "occasion",
  iterations = 20000, warmup = 10000, thin = 10, chains = 2
)
summary(satisfaction)
@
%
The summary lists the coefficients, the free thresholds \code{gamma[2]} to
\code{gamma[4]}, and the mean and the variance of the random intercept,
\code{mu[ASC]} and \code{Omega[ASC,ASC]}. The first threshold is fixed at zero
and the error variance at one. According to the model, the probability of a
higher satisfaction level decreases with age and with the degree of handicap and
increases with income and for married persons. With a random intercept variance
of 0.86 and an error variance of one, persistent differences between persons
account for about 46 percent of the unexplained variation in the latent utility.
The repeated responses of the same persons identify these differences, which
capture determinants that the covariates omit, such as chronic conditions or
individual standards of judging one's health. Figure~\ref{fig:thresholds} shows
how the thresholds translate the latent utility of a married person with mean
income and no handicap into the five levels: an age of 64 instead of 25 years
shifts the density to the left and moves probability from the two highest to the
two lowest levels. The thresholds are unequally spaced: level 2 covers the
widest bounded interval of the latent utility and is the most probable level at
both ages, whereas level 3 covers a narrow interval and is less probable than
the highest level at 25 years. Section~\ref{sec:new_covariates} predicts the
probability of the highest level over the age range of the sample.
%
<<ordered-thresholds, fig=TRUE, echo=FALSE, width=6, height=2.5>>=
satisfaction_coef <- coef(satisfaction)
intercept_variance <- satisfaction_coef[["Omega[ASC,ASC]"]]
utility_young <- satisfaction_coef[["mu[ASC]"]] +
  min(Health$age) * satisfaction_coef[["beta[age]"]] +
  satisfaction_coef[["beta[married]"]]
utility_old <- utility_young + (64 - 25) * satisfaction_coef[["beta[age]"]]
utility_sd <- sqrt(1 + intercept_variance)
thresholds <- c(0, satisfaction_coef[c("gamma[2]", "gamma[3]", "gamma[4]")])
utility_grid <- seq(-2, 8, length.out = 400)
op <- par(mar = c(4, 4, 1.5, 0.5))
plot(
  utility_grid, dnorm(utility_grid, utility_young, utility_sd),
  type = "l", lwd = 2, xlab = "latent utility", ylab = "density"
)
lines(
  utility_grid, dnorm(utility_grid, utility_old, utility_sd),
  lwd = 2, lty = 2
)
abline(v = thresholds, lty = 3)
mtext(
  0:4, side = 3, line = 0.2,
  at = c(-1, (thresholds[-1] + thresholds[-4]) / 2, thresholds[4] + 1)
)
legend(
  "topleft", legend = c("25 years", "64 years"),
  lwd = 2, lty = 1:2, bty = "n"
)
par(op)
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics{ordered-thresholds}
\caption{Density of the latent utility of a married person with mean income
and no handicap at 25 years (solid) and 64 years (dashed) of age. The dotted
lines mark the thresholds $\gamma_1 = 0$
and $\gamma_2$ to $\gamma_4$, and the areas under a density between the
thresholds are the probabilities of the five levels.}
\label{fig:thresholds}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Ranked responses} \label{sec:ranked}

The \code{Game} data of the \pkg{mlogit} package \citep{Fok2012} record the
preferences of 91 Dutch respondents for six gaming platforms: Xbox, PlayStation,
PSPortable, GameCube, GameBoy, and PC. Every respondent ranks all six platforms
from the most to the least preferred. Platform ownership (\code{own}) enters the
formula as a covariate of type \code{A}, and the weekly hours of gaming
(\code{hours}) and the age of the respondent (\code{age}) as covariates of type
\code{B}, whose coefficients are interpreted relative to the GameBoy as the base
alternative. The respondents are between 17 and 33 years old, with a median of
20 years, and play a median of 2 hours per week. Almost all of them own a PC (80
of 91), whereas between 8 and 29 respondents own each of the other platforms,
and half of the respondents own a single platform. The PC is ranked first by 39
respondents, the PlayStation and the Xbox by 18 each, the GameCube and the
PSPortable by 7 each, and the GameBoy by only 2. For the comparison at the end
of this section, we store the first-ranked platform of every respondent in the
column \code{first}:
%
<<ranked-fit>>=
data("Game", package = "mlogit")
rank_columns <- grep("^ch\\.", names(Game), value = TRUE)
Game$first <- sub("ch.", "", rank_columns, fixed = TRUE)[
  apply(Game[rank_columns], 1, which.min)
]
gaming_rank <- fit(
  ch ~ own | hours + age, data = Game, choice_type = "ranked",
  delimiter = ".", column_decider = NULL,
  iterations = 40000, warmup = 20000, thin = 20, chains = 2
)
@
%
Figure~\ref{fig:gaming_intervals} shows the marginal posteriors of the
ownership, hours, and age coefficients, drawn by \fct{plot} with
\code{type = "interval"}:
%
<<ranked-interval, fig=TRUE, width=6, height=3>>=
plot(
  gaming_rank, type = "interval", prob_outer = 0.95,
  variables = grep(
    "own|hours|age", names(coef(gaming_rank)), value = TRUE
  )
)
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics{ranked-interval}
\caption{Marginal posterior distributions of the ownership coefficient and of
the hours and age coefficients relative to the GameBoy in the ranked model
\code{gaming\_rank}: posterior medians (points), 50\% credible intervals (thick
lines), and 95\% credible intervals (thin lines).}
\label{fig:gaming_intervals}
\end{figure}
%
According to the model, respondents rank the platforms they own higher than
those they do not own. The ownership coefficient has a posterior mean of
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", coef(gaming_rank)[["beta[own]"]])} and a 95\% credible
interval from 0.53 to 1.20. Respondents who play more hours rank the PC and, to
a lesser extent, the PlayStation higher relative to the GameBoy: the posterior
mean of the hours coefficient is 0.20 for the PC and 0.07 for the PlayStation,
that is, one more hour of gaming per week raises their utility relative to the
GameBoy by these amounts, whereas it lies between $-0.01$ and 0.04 for the other
three platforms. Older respondents rank the PC slightly higher, with a posterior
mean of 0.06 per year for its age coefficient and between 0.00 and 0.03 for the
other platforms.

To quantify the information that the complete rankings add to the first choices
(Section~\ref{sec:model:ordered_ranked}), we fit the same specification to the
first-ranked platforms as an unordered choice:
%
<<ranked-first>>=
gaming_first <- update(
  gaming_rank, first ~ own | hours + age, choice_type = "unordered"
)
@
%
The posterior means and standard deviations of the coefficients under both
models are:
%
<<ranked-first-sd>>=
gaming_comparison <- merge(
  summary(gaming_rank)$posterior[, c("variable", "mean", "sd")],
  summary(gaming_first)$posterior[, c("variable", "mean", "sd")],
  by = "variable", suffixes = c("_ranked", "_first")
)
print(
  subset(gaming_comparison, grepl("^beta", variable)),
  digits = 2, row.names = FALSE
)
@
%
Although both models use the responses of the same 91 respondents, the posterior
standard deviations are up to 5 times larger with the first choices than with
the rankings. The hours effect of the PC, whose 95\% credible interval excludes
zero under the rankings, is no longer distinguishable from zero under the first
choices. In this application, the complete rankings thus yield considerably more
precise estimates than the first choices alone.

\section{Prediction and residuals} \label{sec:prediction}

The \fct{predict} method computes posterior predictive choice probabilities,
whose deviations from the observed choices define the residuals of
Section~\ref{sec:residuals}. The population choice probability integrates the
choice probability of Section~\ref{sec:model:probabilities} over the population
distribution of $\theta_n$,
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:population_probability}
\Prob(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, \vartheta)
= \int \Prob(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, \theta_n, \tilde{\Sigma})\,
  p(\theta_n \mid \vartheta)\, \mathrm{d}\theta_n,
\end{equation}
%
where $p(\theta_n \mid \vartheta)$ is the distribution of $\theta_n$ implied by
(\ref{eq:random}), (\ref{eq:mixture}), and (\ref{eq:partition}). The
population posterior predictive probability averages
(\ref{eq:population_probability}) over the posterior,
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:posterior_predictive}
\Prob^{\mathrm{pop}}(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, y)
= \int \Prob(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, \vartheta)\, p(\vartheta \mid y)\,
  \mathrm{d}\vartheta
\approx \frac{1}{D} \sum_{d = 1}^{D}
  \Prob\big(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, \vartheta^{(d)}\big).
\end{equation}

\subsection{Population and conditional predictions} \label{sec:conditional}

For a decider $n$ in the sample, \code{type = "conditional"} replaces the
population distribution by the posterior draws of the decider's own coefficients
and class allocation (Section~\ref{sec:individual}),
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:conditional_predictive}
\Prob^{\mathrm{cond}}(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, y)
\approx \frac{1}{D} \sum_{d = 1}^{D}
  \Prob\big(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, \theta_n^{(d)},
  \tilde{\Sigma}^{(d)}\big),
\end{equation}
%
and thus exploits the choice history of the decider.

We compare both types of prediction for a repeated binary decision in an online
chess tournament. In arena tournaments on the chess server Lichess, a player may
go ``Berserk'' at the start of every game: the player's clock is halved, and a
win earns one extra tournament point. Going Berserk is thus a gamble that trades
a higher risk of losing for a larger reward in case of a win. The
\code{lichess_berserk_choice} data of \pkg{choicedata} record this decision for
the players of the Lichess Yearly Rapid Arena of April 2026, together with the
playing color, the player's rating, the rating difference to the opponent, the
remaining tournament time, and whether the player was on a winning streak, which
doubles the points of a win. We standardize the three numeric covariates, and
\fct{train\_test} holds out one randomly chosen game per player:
%
<<berserk-data>>=
data("lichess_berserk_choice", package = "choicedata")
berserk_data <- as.data.frame(lichess_berserk_choice)
berserk_data <- transform(
  berserk_data, rating = c(scale(rating)),
  ratingDifference = c(scale(ratingDifference)),
  minutesRemaining = c(scale(minutesRemaining))
)
berserk_split <- train_test(
  berserk_data, test_number = 1, by = "occasion", random = TRUE,
  column_occasion = "occasionID"
)
sapply(berserk_split, nrow)
@
%
<<berserk-games-figure, fig=TRUE, echo=FALSE, width=6, height=1.9>>=
games_per_player <- table(berserk_data$deciderID)
op <- par(mar = c(4, 4, 0.5, 0.5))
hist(
  games_per_player, breaks = 0:max(games_per_player), col = "gray85",
  border = "white", main = "", xlab = "games per player", ylab = "players"
)
par(op)
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics{berserk-games-figure}
\caption{Number of games per player in the \code{lichess\_berserk\_choice}
data.}
\label{fig:berserk_games}
\end{figure}
%
The data contain \Sexpr{nrow(berserk_data)} games of
\Sexpr{nrow(berserk_split$test)} players, between 1 and
\Sexpr{max(table(berserk_data$deciderID))} per player.
Figure~\ref{fig:berserk_games} shows that most players play only a few games,
with a median of \Sexpr{median(table(berserk_data$deciderID))} games per
player. Players may differ in their inclination to go Berserk beyond what the
covariates explain. We therefore fit the model without and with a random effect
on the ASC, which represents this inclination as a normally distributed
propensity of every player, and save the individual draws for the conditional
predictions:
%
<<berserk-fit>>=
berserk_fixed <- fit(
  berserk ~ 0 | white + rating + ratingDifference + minutesRemaining +
    streak,
  data = berserk_split$train, column_occasion = "occasionID",
  iterations = 1000, warmup = 500, chains = 2
)
berserk <- update(
  berserk_fixed, random_effects = "ASC", iterations = 10000,
  warmup = 5000, thin = 50, save_individual_draws = TRUE
)
round(cbind(mean = coef(berserk), confint(berserk, level = 0.9)), 3)
@
%
According to the model, players go Berserk more often with the white pieces,
which have the advantage of the first move, when their rating is high, when it
exceeds that of the opponent, and late in the tournament, while a winning streak
has no clear effect. Among the standardized covariates, the rating difference
clearly has the strongest effect. The negative mean \code{mu[ASC_TRUE]} of the
propensity reflects that 74 percent of the games are played without Berserk, and
the propensity varies considerably between players, as indicated by the variance
\code{Omega[ASC_TRUE,ASC_TRUE]} of about
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", coef(berserk)[["Omega[ASC_TRUE,ASC_TRUE]"]])}. The model
without a random effect predicts each hold-out game by its population
probability, whereas the conditional prediction of \code{berserk} uses the
player's other games:
%
<<holdout>>=
known <- berserk_split$test$deciderID %in%
  berserk_split$train$deciderID
holdout <- berserk_split$test[known, ]
holdout_fixed <- predict(berserk_fixed, newdata = holdout)
holdout_conditional <- predict(
  berserk, newdata = holdout, type = "conditional"
)
head(holdout_conditional, 3)
@
%
\fct{predict} returns a data frame with one row per choice occasion, with the
most probable alternative in \code{.prediction} and the posterior predictive
probabilities in \code{probability_*}. Excluding the 1313 players with a single
game leaves \Sexpr{nrow(holdout)} hold-out games. For these games, the share of
correct predictions is 78.3 percent for the model without a random effect,
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", 100 * mean(!holdout$berserk))} percent for the naive rule
that never predicts Berserk, and 88.8 percent for the conditional predictions.
Figure~\ref{fig:roc} shows the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves of
both predictions, drawn with \pkg{plotROC} \citep{Sachs2017} and \pkg{ggplot2}
\citep{Wickham2016}. The area under the curve is 0.92 for the conditional
predictions and 0.68 for the model without a random effect. The conditional
predictions are thus considerably better.
%
<<roc-curve, fig=TRUE, echo=FALSE, width=3.6, height=3.6>>=
library("ggplot2")
library("plotROC")
roc_data <- rbind(
  data.frame(
    prediction = "conditional", berserk = holdout$berserk,
    probability = holdout_conditional$probability_TRUE
  ),
  data.frame(
    prediction = "without random effect", berserk = holdout$berserk,
    probability = holdout_fixed$probability_TRUE
  )
)
roc_plot <- ggplot(
  roc_data,
  aes(d = as.integer(berserk), m = probability, linetype = prediction)
) +
  geom_roc(n.cuts = 0) +
  style_roc(major.breaks = seq(0, 1, 0.25), minor.breaks = NULL) +
  theme(
    legend.position = "inside", legend.position.inside = c(0.65, 0.15),
    legend.title = element_blank()
  )
print(roc_plot)
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.41\textwidth]{roc-curve}
\caption{Receiver operating characteristic curves for the hold-out games of the
players with at least two games: conditional predictions of the model with a
random effect on the ASC (solid) and population predictions of the model without
a random effect (dashed).}
\label{fig:roc}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Predictions for new covariate values} \label{sec:new_covariates}

With \code{newdata}, \fct{predict} computes the probabilities at arbitrary
covariate values, and \code{uncertainty = TRUE} adds the posterior standard
deviation and an equal-tailed credible interval of every probability, at the
default level of 95\%, which the argument \code{level} changes. For the ordered
model \code{satisfaction} of Section~\ref{sec:ordered}, we predict the
satisfaction of a married person with mean income over the age range of the
sample, without a handicap and with a handicap of 50 percent. These are
population predictions, which integrate over the random intercept. Since age is
centered, the grid value zero is the mean age of 43.5 years, and the sequence
covers the ages from 25 to 64 years in steps of one year. The suffix of the
columns \code{probability_4}, \code{lower_4}, and \code{upper_4} refers to the
highest level. The last command prints its probability and credible interval at
the mean age, without and with the handicap:
%
<<ordered-grid>>=
health_grid <- expand.grid(
  age = c(0, seq(min(Health$age), max(Health$age))), income = 0,
  handper = c(0, 50), married = 1
)
grid_prediction <- predict(
  satisfaction, newdata = health_grid, uncertainty = TRUE
)
grid_prediction <- cbind(health_grid, grid_prediction)
subset(
  grid_prediction, age == 0,
  c(handper, probability_4, lower_4, upper_4)
)
@
%
At the mean age and income, the posterior predictive probability of the highest
level is 0.12 without and 0.05 with a handicap of 50 percent.
Figure~\ref{fig:satisfaction} shows the probabilities and their credible
intervals over the age range. Without a handicap, the probability falls from
0.21 at 25 years to 0.05 at 64 years, and the handicap reduces it by more than
half at every age.
%
<<ordered-prediction-figure, fig=TRUE, echo=FALSE, width=6, height=2.9>>=
op <- par(mar = c(4, 4, 0.5, 0.5))
plot(
  NA, xlim = c(25, 64), ylim = c(0, 0.25), xlab = "age in years",
  ylab = "probability of the highest level"
)
for (handicap in c(0, 50)) {
  rows <- which(grid_prediction$handper == handicap & grid_prediction$age != 0)
  years <- 25:64
  polygon(
    c(years, rev(years)),
    c(grid_prediction$lower_4[rows], rev(grid_prediction$upper_4[rows])),
    col = grey(0, alpha = 0.15), border = NA
  )
  lines(
    years, grid_prediction$probability_4[rows], lwd = 2,
    lty = if (handicap == 0) 1 else 2
  )
}
legend(
  "topright", legend = c("no handicap", "handicap of 50 percent"),
  lwd = 2, lty = 1:2, bty = "n"
)
par(op)
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics{ordered-prediction-figure}
\caption{Posterior predictive probability of the highest satisfaction level by
age for a married person with mean income, without a handicap (solid) and with a
handicap of 50 percent (dashed), with 95\% credible intervals (shaded).}
\label{fig:satisfaction}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Residuals} \label{sec:residuals}

The \fct{residuals} method returns the observed choice indicators minus the
posterior predictive probabilities of (\ref{eq:posterior_predictive}),
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:residuals}
e_{ntj} = 1(y_{nt} = j)
  - \Prob^{\mathrm{pop}}(y_{nt} = j \mid X_{nt}, y),
\qquad j = 1, \dots, J,
\end{equation}
%
as a matrix with one row per choice occasion and one column per alternative. The
residuals of an occasion sum to zero, and with two alternatives, they differ
only in sign. A small residual of the chosen alternative indicates an accurately
predicted choice, whereas an absolute value close to one indicates a choice that
the model deems unlikely. The row names of the residual matrix combine the
decider and the choice occasion. For the first three choices of the first
traveler in \code{model\_train}, the residuals are:
%
<<residuals>>=
train_residuals <- residuals(model_train)
head(train_residuals, 3)
@

\section{Model evaluation} \label{sec:evaluation}

For the comparison of competing specifications, \pkg{RprobitB} provides three
criteria: the widely applicable information criterion (WAIC) of
\citet{Watanabe2010}, leave-one-out cross-validation approximated by
Pareto-smoothed importance sampling (PSIS-LOO) of \citet{Vehtari2017,
Vehtari2024}, and Bayes factors \citep{Kass1995}. The first two estimate the
expected predictive accuracy of a model for new deciders, whereas the third
compares the marginal likelihoods of two models. We write $p_n(\vartheta) =
p(y_n \mid X_n, \vartheta)$ for the likelihood contribution of decider $n$.
Since WAIC and PSIS-LOO evaluate $p_n$ under every retained draw, their cost
grows with the number of draws and deciders. Thinning or a parallel \pkg{future}
plan can be used to reduce the computational load.

We compare the three train models in \code{models}
(Section~\ref{sec:mixing_distributions}), whose price coefficient is fixed in
\code{model\_train}, normal in \code{price\_normal}, and negative log-normal in
\code{price\_lognormal}.

\subsection{Log-likelihood} \label{sec:loglik}

\fct{logLik} evaluates the log-likelihood $\sum_{n} \log p_n(\bar{\vartheta})$
at the posterior means $\bar{\vartheta}$ of the population parameters and
returns a \class{logLik} object, which \fct{AIC} and \fct{BIC} accept:
%
<<loglik>>=
sapply(models, logLik)
@
%
The random price coefficient, which adds one free parameter to the five of
\code{model\_train}, raises the log-likelihood by 159 under the normal and by
153 under the negative log-normal mixing distribution. Since \fct{logLik},
\fct{AIC}, and \fct{BIC} rely on a single point estimate, they ignore the
posterior uncertainty about $\vartheta$, unlike WAIC and PSIS-LOO.

\subsection{WAIC and PSIS-LOO} \label{sec:loo}

Both criteria estimate the expected log predictive density (elpd) of a model for
the responses $\tilde{y}_n$ of new deciders,
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:elpd}
\mathrm{elpd} = \sum_{n = 1}^{N} \mathbb{E}\big[\log p(\tilde{y}_n \mid X_n,
y)\big], \qquad
p(\tilde{y}_n \mid X_n, y) = \int p(\tilde{y}_n \mid X_n, \vartheta)\,
p(\vartheta \mid y)\, \mathrm{d}\vartheta,
\end{equation}
%
which increases with the predictive accuracy. WAIC estimates the elpd as
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:waic}
\widehat{\mathrm{elpd}}_{\mathrm{waic}} = \sum_{n = 1}^{N} \log \bigg(
\frac{1}{D} \sum_{d = 1}^{D} p_n\big(\vartheta^{(d)}\big) \bigg) - \sum_{n =
1}^{N} \mathrm{Var}_d\big[\log p_n(\vartheta^{(d)})\big], \qquad
\mathrm{WAIC} = -2\, \widehat{\mathrm{elpd}}_{\mathrm{waic}},
\end{equation}
%
where $\mathrm{Var}_d$ is the sample variance over the $D$ draws. PSIS-LOO
instead estimates the leave-one-out predictive probability $p(y_n \mid y_{-n})$
of the responses of decider $n$ given the responses $y_{-n}$ of all other
deciders by importance sampling over the retained draws,
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:psis_loo}
\widehat{\mathrm{elpd}}_{\mathrm{loo}} = \sum_{n = 1}^{N} \log \hat{p}(y_n \mid
y_{-n}), \qquad
\hat{p}(y_n \mid y_{-n}) = \frac{\sum_{d = 1}^{D} w_n^{(d)}\,
p_n(\vartheta^{(d)})}{\sum_{d = 1}^{D} w_n^{(d)}},
\end{equation}
%
where the weights $w_n^{(d)}$ are Pareto-smoothed importance ratios. Both
estimates are sums of pointwise contributions $\widehat{\mathrm{elpd}}_n$. The
standard error of the difference between two models $\mathcal{M}_1$ and
$\mathcal{M}_2$ follows from the variance of the pointwise differences,
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:elpd_difference}
\Delta_n = \widehat{\mathrm{elpd}}_{n}(\mathcal{M}_1) -
\widehat{\mathrm{elpd}}_{n}(\mathcal{M}_2),
\qquad
\mathrm{se}\Big(\sum_{n = 1}^{N} \Delta_n\Big) = \sqrt{N\,
\mathrm{Var}_n(\Delta_n)},
\end{equation}
%
with $\mathrm{Var}_n$ the sample variance over the $N$ deciders
\citep{Vehtari2017}. \fct{WAIC} and \fct{loo} pass the $D \times N$ matrix of
the decider log-likelihoods to the \pkg{loo} package and return the objects of
that package. For \code{model_train}, \fct{WAIC} gives
$\widehat{\mathrm{elpd}}_{\mathrm{waic}} = -1707.0$ with a standard error of
40.2. For four of the travelers, the variance term exceeds $0.4$, the level
above which \citet{Vehtari2017} consider the WAIC approximation unreliable.
PSIS-LOO is then preferable, because it is more robust in such cases.
\fct{loo\_compare} ranks the three models by
$\widehat{\mathrm{elpd}}_{\mathrm{loo}}$:
%
<<loo>>=
train_loo <- lapply(models, loo)
loo_comparison <- loo::loo_compare(train_loo)
loo_comparison
@
%
The column \code{p\_worse} gives the probability that a model has a lower elpd
than the first-ranked one. The normal mixing distribution ranks first, but the
elpd under the negative log-normal mixing distribution is lower by only 4.3 with
a standard error of 10.0. The data therefore do not clearly favor either mixing
distribution. Under a fixed price coefficient, in contrast, the elpd is lower by
159 with a standard error of 25. A random price coefficient thus clearly
improves the prediction of a traveler's choices. According to
Figure~\ref{fig:elpd_differences}, the random price coefficient raises the elpd
contribution of 60 percent of the travelers.
%
<<elpd-differences-figure, fig=TRUE, echo=FALSE, width=6, height=3.5>>=
elpd_difference <- train_loo$price_normal$pointwise[, "elpd_loo"] -
  train_loo$model_train$pointwise[, "elpd_loo"]
op <- par(mar = c(4, 4, 0.5, 0.5))
plot(
  sort(elpd_difference), type = "h", col = "gray60",
  xlab = "travelers, ordered by the difference",
  ylab = "difference in elpd per traveler"
)
abline(h = 0)
abline(h = mean(elpd_difference), lty = 2)
par(op)
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics{elpd-differences-figure}
\caption{Pointwise differences $\Delta_n$ of (\ref{eq:elpd_difference})
in the leave-one-out expected log predictive density between
\code{price\_normal} and \code{model\_train}, one vertical line per traveler,
ordered by size. Positive values favor \code{price\_normal}. The dashed line
marks the mean difference.}
\label{fig:elpd_differences}
\end{figure}
%
The same comparison applies to the number of latent classes. For the
\code{Electricity} data, we compare the model \code{electricity} of
Section~\ref{sec:random_coefficients}, in which the valuation of a local
supplier follows a single normal distribution, with the two-class model
\code{electricity\_classes} of Section~\ref{sec:latent_classes}:
%
<<loo-classes, eval=FALSE>>=
classes_loo <- lapply(mget(c("electricity", "electricity_classes")), loo)
classes_comparison <- loo::loo_compare(classes_loo)
classes_comparison
@
%
Running this code takes more than ten minutes. The elpd of the one-class model
is lower by 16.8 with a standard error of 5.2, which favors the two classes.

\subsection{Bayes factors} \label{sec:bayes_factors}

A Bayes factor compares two models $\mathcal{M}_1$ and $\mathcal{M}_2$ by the
ratio of their marginal likelihoods,
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:bayes_factor}
\mathrm{BF}_{12} = \frac{p(y \mid \mathcal{M}_1)}{p(y \mid \mathcal{M}_2)},
\qquad
p(y \mid \mathcal{M}) = \int \prod_{n = 1}^{N} p(y_n \mid X_n, \vartheta,
\mathcal{M})\, p(\vartheta \mid \mathcal{M})\, \mathrm{d}\vartheta,
\end{equation}
%
where $p(\vartheta \mid \mathcal{M})$ is the prior of
Section~\ref{sec:est_prior}. \citet{Kass1995} interpret values of
$2 \log \mathrm{BF}_{12}$ above $10$ as very strong evidence for
$\mathcal{M}_1$. \fct{bayes\_factor} estimates the marginal likelihoods by
bridge sampling \citep{Meng1996, FruehwirthSchnatter2004, Gronau2017}.
%
<<bf>>=
log_bf <- bayes_factor(price_normal, model_train, log = TRUE)
log_bf
@
%
The log Bayes factor of \Sexpr{round(log_bf$bf)}, that is, $2 \log
\mathrm{BF}_{12} \approx \Sexpr{round(2 * log_bf$bf)}$, is very strong evidence
for the normal mixing distribution over the fixed price coefficient, in line
with the comparison by PSIS-LOO.

\section{Summary} \label{sec:summary}

The \proglang{R} package \pkg{RprobitB} implements the Bayesian estimation of
probit models for binary, multinomial, ordered, and ranked responses. All models
are specified through the single function \fct{fit}, whose formula interface
distinguishes the three covariate types of discrete choice data. The error
covariance is estimated, which permits flexible substitution patterns, and
preference heterogeneity is modeled through random coefficients with normal or
log-normal mixing distributions, through latent classes, or through both. The
number of latent classes can be fixed in advance or inferred from the data. For
efficient computation, the Gibbs sampler is implemented in \proglang{C++} and
can run several chains in parallel.

Since the derived quantities are evaluated under all retained draws, the
posterior uncertainty propagates to willingness-to-pay measures, marginal
effects, predicted choice probabilities, and the coefficients of individual
deciders. Conditional predictions incorporate the choice history of a decider,
and the posterior distribution of the number of latent classes quantifies the
uncertainty about the number of segments. Together with the standard methods of
\proglang{R} and the model comparison, the package covers a full analysis
workflow.

The package is intended for applied researchers and practitioners in
transportation, marketing, health, and related fields who analyze discrete
choice data and who report willingness-to-pay measures, marginal effects, or
predictions together with their uncertainty. Suggestions are welcome as issues
at \url{https://github.com/loelschlaeger/RprobitB/issues}.

\section*{Computational details}

The results in this paper were obtained using
\proglang{R}~\Sexpr{paste(R.Version()[c("major", "minor")], collapse = ".")}
with the packages \pkg{RprobitB}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("RprobitB")} and
\pkg{choicedata}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("choicedata")} under
\Sexpr{utils::osVersion}. The data sets of the examples come from
\pkg{mlogit}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("mlogit")},
\pkg{Rchoice}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("Rchoice")}, and \pkg{choicedata}.
\pkg{RprobitB} builds on \pkg{oeli}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("oeli")}
\citep{Oelschlaeger2026b}, \pkg{Rcpp}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("Rcpp")}, and
\pkg{RcppArmadillo}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("RcppArmadillo")} for the sampler and
on \pkg{Formula}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("Formula")} for the formula interface.
The retained draws are stored with
\pkg{posterior}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("posterior")} and displayed with
\pkg{bayesplot}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("bayesplot")}, and model evaluation uses
\pkg{loo}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("loo")} and
\pkg{bridgesampling}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("bridgesampling")}. The ROC curves
are drawn with \pkg{plotROC}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("plotROC")} and
\pkg{ggplot2}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("ggplot2")}. Parallel chains and progress
reporting use \pkg{future}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("future")},
\pkg{future.apply}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("future.apply")}
\citep{Bengtsson2026b}, and \pkg{progressr}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("progressr")}
\citep{Bengtsson2026}. The chains and the likelihood evaluations of \fct{loo}
ran in parallel on two workers under a multisession plan of \pkg{future}.
\proglang{R} itself and all packages used are available from the Comprehensive
\proglang{R} Archive Network (CRAN) at \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/}.

<<cleanup, echo=FALSE, results=hide>>=
future::plan(future::sequential)
@

